      <section id="introduction" class="section">
        <p>The <strong>aerosol particle size distribution</strong> determines the transport dynamics, surface coverage efficiency, and safety profile of pressurized spray products. It works by categorizing suspended liquid droplets or solid particles based on their physical or aerodynamic diameter across a multi-stage spectrum. This matters for chemical and process engineers because sizing characteristics dictate whether a formulation deposits effectively on target substrates or remains suspended as a respirable hazard. For a foundational exploration of actuator orifice design and its direct influence on atomization physics, consult our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-actuators-101" target="_top">aerosol actuators 101</a>.</p>
        <p>Developing effective aerosol products requires analyzing droplet sizes and spray angles to verify formulation efficacy and avoid inhalation risks. Physical properties of propellants, solvent density, and valve elastomer selections play a massive role in droplet breaking mechanics. This guide breaks down the methods and sizing metrics used in modern aerosol engineering labs.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="principles" class="section">
        <h2>Introduction to Aerosol Particle Sizing and Characterization</h2>
        <p>Aerosol particle size characterization is a key engineering process for many industries, including cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, automotive coatings, and household cleaners. When a pressurized formulation is discharged, the high-velocity expansion of propellants causes the liquid stream to break up into a cloud of millions of droplets. Understanding how these droplets behave in flight requires measuring their size distribution profile using precise optical and mechanical instruments. To explore general scientific literature and aerosol definition physics, see the comprehensive <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerosol" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia aerosol science summary</a>.</p>
        <p>Logistics and quality control systems rely on repeatable particle sizing to ensure product consistency and meet strict regulatory standards. Droplet size profiles affect not only the sensory characteristics of consumer sprays (like spray dry-time and feel) but also their safety profiles. Inhalation hazards are directly linked to particle size, making size validation a non-negotiable compliance step. To review standard chemical solvents, binders, and active ingredients that influence spray atomization, see our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-chemistry-101" target="_top">aerosol chemistry 101</a>.</p>
      </section>

