      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="levels" class="section">
        <h2>The Physics of Droplet Size Aerosol Dispersion</h2>
        <p>The final droplet size aerosol distribution of a spray is determined by the balance of cohesive forces within the formulation and destructive aerodynamic forces at the nozzle exit. Viscosity, surface tension, and liquid density resist droplet breakup, while propellant expansion and shear forces disrupt the liquid sheet. The Weber number (We), a dimensionless parameter comparing inertial forces to surface tension, is used to model this breakup behavior. The British Aerosol Manufacturers' Association maintains reports on droplet behaviors and spray physics in the official <a href="https://www.bama.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BAMA technical resources</a> portal.</p>
        <p>If the formulation viscosity is too high, or if the propellant pressure is insufficient, the atomization process will fail, resulting in large droplets, spitting, or stream discharge. Conversely, low viscosity and high propellant levels generate highly atomized sprays with very small droplets. Formulators must balance these parameters to optimize spray patterns while avoiding the generation of respirable fine particles. To review safety systems, canister margins, and flammability testing parameters, read our complete guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-can-safety-explained" target="_top">aerosol can safety explained</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="sprinklers" class="section">
        <h2>Laser Diffraction Aerosol Measurement Principles</h2>
        <p>Laser diffraction aerosol measurement is the most common optical method used to characterize industrial and consumer sprays. This technique passes a collimated laser beam through the spray plume, where droplets scatter the light at angles inversely proportional to their size. Large droplets scatter light at narrow angles with high intensity, while small droplets scatter light at wider angles with lower intensity. The scattered light pattern is captured by an array of photosensors, and mathematical algorithms calculate the volumetric size distribution.</p>
        <p>The value of laser diffraction is its speed, allowing real-time measurement of transient spray phases (such as the start, middle, and end of a spray actuation). This method is non-intrusive, meaning it does not alter the droplet velocities or trajectories during sizing. However, it requires careful nozzle positioning relative to the laser beam, as dense plumes can cause multiple scattering events that distort the results. Laser diffraction instruments must be calibrated using standard reference materials under stable temperature and humidity conditions.</p>

