      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="compliance" class="section">
        <h2>Cascade Impactor Aerosol Testing and Sizing Stages</h2>
        <p>For pharmaceutical aerosols like metered-dose inhalers (MDIs), cascade impactor aerosol testing is the primary regulatory standard. Unlike optical methods, cascade impactors separate particles based on their aerodynamic behavior, which directly correlates with how droplets deposit in the human lungs. The aerosol is drawn through the impactor stages at a set flow rate. Each stage features smaller nozzle jets that increase the air velocity, forcing larger particles to impact on collection plates while smaller particles flow to subsequent stages.</p>
        <p>After sampling, the mass of the formulation deposited on each stage is quantified using high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) or gravimetric analysis. This allows formulators to determine the active ingredient concentration within specific sizing ranges. While cascade impaction is time-consuming and labor-intensive, it provides the chemical specificity required for regulatory filings. Researchers can find validation studies for inhalation and impactor testing protocols in the peer-reviewed <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed database</a>.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          Cascade impactors measure the aerodynamic diameter rather than physical size. For porous or low-density droplets, the aerodynamic diameter can be significantly smaller than the physical diameter, affecting deposition velocity calculations.
        </div>
      </section>

