      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="chemistry" class="section">
        <h2>Sizing Parameters: MMAD Aerosol Spray Diagnostics</h2>
        <p>Analyzing size distributions requires defining key statistical parameters to describe the spray cloud. The primary metric used is the MMAD aerosol spray diameter, which is the Mass Median Aerodynamic Diameter. The MMAD represents the aerodynamic diameter above and below which exactly 50% of the total aerosol mass is suspended. This value is used to assess respirable risk, as particles with an MMAD smaller than 10 microns can enter the respiratory tract. Sizing statistics can be cross-referenced against standard physical databases like the <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM International standards database</a>.</p>
        <p>Other critical sizing parameters include the Span, which measures the width of the size distribution curve. A narrow Span indicates a highly uniform droplet size, while a wide Span indicates a broad range of droplet sizes. The Dv10 and Dv90 values represent the diameters below which 10% and 90% of the spray volume exist. Monitoring these values during product development helps engineers optimize valve and actuator geometries to ensure a consistent, safe spray pattern.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          For consumer products like hair sprays or insect repellents, formulators must keep the respirable fraction (droplets &lt; 10 microns) below 1% of the total spray volume to prevent inhalation health risks.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="design" class="section">
        <h2>Practical Aerosol Spray Characterization for Quality Systems</h2>
        <p>Integrating aerosol spray characterization into a manufacturing quality system requires standardized testing protocols. Small changes in nozzle dimensions, propellant pressure, or active ingredient concentrations can significantly alter the spray droplet size. Quality assurance programs use automated laser diffraction systems to test samples from each production run, verifying that the Dv50 and Span remain within specifications. This step ensures consistent spray performance and safety across batches.</p>
        <p>Environmental parameters like temperature and humidity must be controlled during sizing tests, as they affect propellant evaporation rates and droplet lifetimes. Test benches must feature exhaust systems to capture the spray, preventing droplet recirculation and lens contamination. By maintaining strict control over testing parameters, manufacturers can generate reliable spray data to support regulatory compliance and product development.</p>
      </section>

