        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is aerosol particle size distribution?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Aerosol particle size distribution describes the relative abundance of aerosol droplets or solid particles categorized by their physical or aerodynamic diameter within a given volume of air. It is a critical parameter determining deposition kinetics, spray coverage efficiency, and respiratory safety boundaries.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How is laser diffraction used to measure aerosol spray droplets?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Laser diffraction aerosol measurement operates by passing a collimated laser beam through the spray plume. Droplets scatter the laser light at angles inversely proportional to their size. Optical detectors collect this scattered light pattern, and mathematical models decode it into a volumetric droplet size distribution.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the purpose of cascade impactor aerosol testing?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Cascade impactor aerosol testing separates particles inertially based on their aerodynamic diameter rather than optical size. By drawing the aerosol through stages of decreasing jet nozzle diameter, particles are forced to impact on specific plates based on their velocity, allowing physical collection and subsequent chemical assaying of different sizing fractions.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What does MMAD stand for in aerosol spray diagnostics?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">MMAD stands for Mass Median Aerodynamic Diameter. It represents the aerodynamic diameter above and below which exactly 50% of the total aerosol mass is suspended. It is the primary metric used by pharmaceutical and industrial formulators to classify inhalable fractions.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does droplet size affect the safety and efficacy of personal care aerosols?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Droplet size determines whether a personal care spray remains on the target skin/hair surface or becomes respirable. Droplets smaller than 10 microns can bypass nasal filtration and enter deep pulmonary alveolar spaces, posing inhalation health risks, which is why cosmetic spray formulators aim to keep mean droplet diameters well above 30 microns.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What standards govern the testing and validation of aerosol spray characterization?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Aerosol spray characterization is validated under standards established by groups like ASTM International (such as ASTM D5964 for spray rates) and the International Organization for Standardization (ISO). These guidelines mandate specific nozzle-to-beam distances, airflow rates, and temperature controls to ensure repeatable size distribution measurements.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

